diagnosis_text:
The helpdesk ticket describes an intermittent link-flapping fault: PC1 and PC2 (10.5.0.1/2, VLAN 5) losing connectivity through their gateway (10.5.0.254) repeatedly and recovering on its own. Per the hard-gate methodology for intermittent/past claims, the fault must be ruled out by its mechanism and traces rather than a single clean snapshot.

A complete four-phase sweep was conducted across all 28 devices in the Kath4 lab. Phase 1 reachability returned 0% loss on all tested paths, including cross-subnet traffic to and from pc1/pc2. Phase A infrastructure sweep found 0 flagged devices, no ACL rules, no ARP anomalies, and all 15 hosts with valid IPs and default routes. Phase B confirmed OSPF fully converged on all 9 routers — sshswitch is correctly functioning as the VLAN-5 gateway (br_v5 = 10.5.0.254/24) with one full OSPF adjacency upstream to router17. No traffic-control shaping or qdisc anomalies were found on any interface. Phase C temporal history for the exact claimed path (pc1 → switch4 → sshswitch) shows zero `network_down` events and zero `network_unreachable` events — no carrier transitions have occurred since lab startup. Phase D pressure sweep found no stress-tool processes, no CPU pressure, and no resource contention on any device.

Root cause: There is no active or historical evidence of link flapping, carrier transitions, or connectivity disruption on the 10.5.0.x path. The reported symptom is unconfirmed. The network is operating normally.

Fix: No corrective action is required. If the users continue to experience disruption, instrument the client side (e.g., continuous ping to 10.5.0.254) to capture a loss event with a timestamp, which would allow a targeted historical trace.
=== END ===